<section class="slide active hero"><div class="tech-hud"><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="data-dot" style="left:8%;top:72%;animation-delay:.7s"></i><i class="data-dot" style="left:48%;top:82%;animation-delay:2.4s"></i></div><div class="tech-ticker"><span>PIPELINE // ACTIVE</span><span>HUMAN VALIDATION // ON</span></div><div class="inner"><div class="tag"><span class="pulse"></span>Risk Data Office · Payments Controls PoC</div><div style="position:absolute;top:-2px;right:0;display:flex;gap:5px;align-items:center" aria-label="Westpac Group colour palette"><i style="width:22px;height:5px;border-radius:9px;background:var(--westpac-red)"></i><i style="width:22px;height:5px;border-radius:9px;background:var(--westpac-navy-mid)"></i><i style="width:22px;height:5px;border-radius:9px;background:var(--westpac-teal)"></i><i style="width:22px;height:5px;border-radius:9px;background:var(--westpac-gold)"></i></div><h1>What if AI could show us where controls overlap, where they fragment, and where coverage disappears?</h1><p class="sub">AI-assisted Payments Controls Rationalisation PoC · connecting JUNO controls to process, product and application context to surface actionable signals.</p><div class="metrics reveal"><div class="metric"><b>708</b><span>identified Payments controls</span></div><div class="metric"><b>808</b><span>identified Payments processes</span></div><div class="metric"><b>163</b><span>controls within fragmentation and consolidation opportunities</span></div></div></div></section>

<section class="slide"><div class="tech-hud"><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="data-dot" style="left:8%;top:72%;animation-delay:.7s"></i><i class="data-dot" style="left:48%;top:82%;animation-delay:2.4s"></i></div><div class="tech-ticker"><span>PIPELINE // ACTIVE</span><span>HUMAN VALIDATION // ON</span></div><div class="inner"><div class="eyebrow">01 · Data foundation</div><h2>Connected data. Incomplete control coverage.</h2><div class="systems reveal"><div class="node red"><div class="name" style="color:var(--red)">JUNO</div><div class="desc">~7,500 controls<br>Control design, objective, nature<br>Risk and obligation relationships</div></div><div class="arrow">→<small>L3 Activity ID</small></div><div class="node gold"><div class="name" style="color:var(--gold)">Holocentric</div><div class="desc">~10,000 activities<br>L2/L3 process hierarchy<br>Process, product and usage context</div></div><div class="arrow">→<small>associated context</small></div><div class="node blue"><div class="name" style="color:var(--blue)">Alfabet</div><div class="desc">Application architecture<br>Associated applications<br>Technology context</div></div></div><div class="cards" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr"><div class="card teal"><strong>Deterministic linkage exists</strong><p>JUNO controls can link to Holocentric through L3 Activity IDs. The challenge is that not every control has that relationship populated.</p></div><div class="card blue"><strong>Context is distributed</strong><p>Rationalisation requires control, process, product and application context to be assessed together rather than relying on control text alone.</p></div></div></div></section>

<section class="slide"><div class="tech-hud"><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="data-dot" style="left:8%;top:72%;animation-delay:.7s"></i><i class="data-dot" style="left:48%;top:82%;animation-delay:2.4s"></i></div><div class="tech-ticker"><span>PIPELINE // ACTIVE</span><span>HUMAN VALIDATION // ON</span></div><div class="inner"><div class="eyebrow">02 · Establishing scope</div><h2>Payments controls were identified using four complementary signals.</h2><div class="signal-grid reveal"><div class="signal"><div class="num">01</div><h3>Risk linkage</h3><p>L3 Risk linked to the Payments Risk Class.</p></div><div class="signal"><div class="num">02</div><h3>Obligation linkage</h3><p>Payments obligations recorded in JUNO.</p></div><div class="signal"><div class="num">03</div><h3>Lexical similarity</h3><p>TF-IDF term-weighted text matching identified Payments language.</p></div><div class="signal"><div class="num">04</div><h3>AI reasoning</h3><p>LLM assessed contextual relevance where deterministic evidence was incomplete.</p></div></div><div class="down">↓</div><div class="result">SME validation → 708 Payments controls</div><p class="note" style="text-align:center">AI augmented deterministic relationships and lexical evidence. It did not define the population by itself.</p></div></section>

<section class="slide"><div class="tech-hud"><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="data-dot" style="left:8%;top:72%;animation-delay:.7s"></i><i class="data-dot" style="left:48%;top:82%;animation-delay:2.4s"></i></div><div class="tech-ticker"><span>PIPELINE // ACTIVE</span><span>HUMAN VALIDATION // ON</span></div><div class="inner"><div class="eyebrow">03 · Establishing scope</div><h2>Payments processes were identified independently.</h2><div class="flow reveal"><div class="node gold"><div class="name">~10,000 activities</div><div class="desc">Holocentric L2/L3 process population</div></div><div class="arrow">→</div><div class="node blue"><div class="name">Selective L2 families</div><div class="desc">Process names used to establish a strong starting population</div></div><div class="arrow">+</div><div class="node purple"><div class="name">LLM classification</div><div class="desc">Contextual assessment of Payments relevance</div></div><div class="arrow">→</div><div class="node teal"><div class="name">808 processes</div><div class="desc">Validated by subject-matter experts</div></div></div><div class="cards" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr"><div class="card"><strong>Control population</strong><p>Identified from JUNO risk, obligation and text evidence.</p></div><div class="card"><strong>Process population</strong><p>Identified from Holocentric process families and contextual classification.</p></div></div></div></section>

<section class="slide"><div class="tech-hud"><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="data-dot" style="left:8%;top:72%;animation-delay:.7s"></i><i class="data-dot" style="left:48%;top:82%;animation-delay:2.4s"></i></div><div class="tech-ticker"><span>PIPELINE // ACTIVE</span><span>HUMAN VALIDATION // ON</span></div><div class="inner"><div class="eyebrow">04 · Analysis pipeline</div><h2>A hybrid analytical pipeline: deterministic joins, vector retrieval and LLM reasoning.</h2><div class="pipeline reveal"><div class="step"><div class="circle">▦</div><h3>Build analytical table</h3><p>Deterministic L3 Activity ID joins plus product and Alfabet application context</p></div><div class="parr">→</div><div class="step"><div class="circle">∿</div><h3>Generate embeddings</h3><p>text-embedding-3-large creates 3,072-dimensional control representations</p></div><div class="parr">→</div><div class="step"><div class="circle">◎</div><h3>Find candidates</h3><p>Cosine similarity ranks candidates within product and CT objective constraints</p></div><div class="parr">→</div><div class="step"><div class="circle">AI</div><h3>Two-pass reasoning</h3><p>Pass 1 assesses each design; Pass 2 compares clusters and selects the strongest pattern</p></div><div class="parr">→</div><div class="step"><div class="circle">✓</div><h3>SME validation</h3><p>Convert model findings into governed decision support</p></div></div><div class="storyline"><span>Joins</span><b>→</b><span>TF-IDF</span><b>→</b><span>Embeddings</span><b>→</b><span>Cosine similarity</span><b>→</b><span>LLM</span><b>→</b><span>SME</span></div><div class="govern"><strong>Human-in-the-loop:</strong> deterministic evidence and analytical signals generate candidates; the LLM explains and recommends; SMEs validate the outcome.</div></div></section>

<section class="slide"><div class="tech-hud"><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="data-dot" style="left:8%;top:72%;animation-delay:.7s"></i><i class="data-dot" style="left:48%;top:82%;animation-delay:2.4s"></i></div><div class="tech-ticker"><span>PIPELINE // ACTIVE</span><span>HUMAN VALIDATION // ON</span></div><div class="inner"><div class="eyebrow">05 · Candidate generation</div><h2>Embedding retrieval turns 708 controls into ranked comparison candidates.</h2><div class="embed reveal"><div><div class="ctrl anchor"><div class="id">CTL000041538 · Anchor</div><div class="cn">LoanIQ: system-enforced maker/checker of payments</div><div class="cd">System-enforced segregation prevents release of payments by the same user.</div></div><div class="ctrl match"><div class="id">CTL000041562</div><div class="cn">SWIFT maker/checker — outbound free-format</div><div class="cd">Same preventative segregation pattern, different platform.</div></div><div class="ctrl match"><div class="id">CTL000041570</div><div class="cn">BankTrade manual payment authorisation</div><div class="cd">Maker/checker for manual payments in BankTrade.</div></div><div class="ctrl"><div class="id">CTL000038201</div><div class="cn">Monthly transaction reporting reconciliation</div><div class="cd">Detective reconciliation with a different control objective.</div></div></div><div class="scorebox"><div class="scoretitle">Illustrative cosine similarity</div><div class="score"><span>CTL…1562</span><div class="barbg"><div class="bar" data-w="92"></div></div><b>92%</b></div><div class="score"><span>CTL…1570</span><div class="barbg"><div class="bar" data-w="87" style="background:var(--gold)"></div></div><b>87%</b></div><div class="score"><span>CTL…8201</span><div class="barbg"><div class="bar" data-w="31" style="background:var(--fg3)"></div></div><b>31%</b></div><p class="note">Illustrative scores are used to explain the method. Similarity creates a review candidate, not a rationalisation decision.</p></div></div></div></section>

<section class="slide"><div class="tech-hud"><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="data-dot" style="left:8%;top:72%;animation-delay:.7s"></i><i class="data-dot" style="left:48%;top:82%;animation-delay:2.4s"></i></div><div class="tech-ticker"><span>PIPELINE // ACTIVE</span><span>HUMAN VALIDATION // ON</span></div><div class="inner"><div class="eyebrow">06 · Structured comparison</div><h2>Objective-aware classification constrains the reasoning space.</h2><div class="ct-layout reveal"><div class="verdict"><div class="vtitle">Example input</div><h3>Payment interface and batch error control</h3><p>Automated and manual controls were assessed separately against objective-specific design principles, coverage and documented nature.</p><div class="finding" style="margin-top:14px"><div class="type">Example classification</div><h3>CT4</h3><p>Payment processing interface and batch error resolution</p></div></div><div class="ct-grid" id="ctgrid"></div></div></div></section>

<section class="slide"><div class="tech-hud"><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="data-dot" style="left:8%;top:72%;animation-delay:.7s"></i><i class="data-dot" style="left:48%;top:82%;animation-delay:2.4s"></i></div><div class="tech-ticker"><span>PIPELINE // ACTIVE</span><span>HUMAN VALIDATION // ON</span></div><div class="inner"><div class="eyebrow">07 · Process context</div><h2>Process metadata tests whether text similarity represents genuine operational overlap.</h2><div class="tax reveal"><div class="taxbox"><h3>Process lifecycle stages</h3><div class="taxitem">Initiation & Validation & Authorisation</div><div class="taxitem">Execution & Early Processing Assurance</div><div class="taxitem">Notification & Reporting</div><div class="taxitem">Clearing / Settlement</div><div class="taxitem active">Posting, Accounting & Detection</div><div class="taxitem">Incident response, disputes, recovery follow-ups</div></div><div class="taxbox"><h3>Process categories</h3><div class="taxitem">Institution to Institution</div><div class="taxitem">Institution to Customer</div><div class="taxitem">Customer to Institution</div><div class="taxitem">Customer to Customer</div><div class="taxitem">Supplier / Contractor / Employee Payments</div><div class="govern" style="margin-top:14px"><strong>Why this matters:</strong> similar control text can have different implications depending on payment flow and lifecycle position.</div></div></div></div></section>

<section class="slide"><div class="tech-hud"><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="data-dot" style="left:8%;top:72%;animation-delay:.7s"></i><i class="data-dot" style="left:48%;top:82%;animation-delay:2.4s"></i></div><div class="tech-ticker"><span>PIPELINE // ACTIVE</span><span>HUMAN VALIDATION // ON</span></div><div class="inner"><div class="eyebrow">08 · Example finding</div><h2>Common Control Opportunity</h2><p class="sub">The same control capability appears across different systems or processes.</p><div class="example reveal"><div class="card purple"><strong>LoanIQ: system-enforced maker/checker of payments</strong><p>CTL000041538 · Trade Finance<br><br>System-enforced segregation prevents release of manual or repaired payments by the same user.</p><span class="chip">Preventative</span><span class="chip">L3 linked</span></div><div class="plus">+</div><div class="card purple"><strong>SWIFT maker/checker — outbound free-format</strong><p>Same system-enforced maker/checker capability applied to a different platform and transaction type.</p><span class="chip">Preventative</span><span class="chip">Nature aligned</span></div></div><div class="finding"><div class="type">Common Control Opportunity</div><h3>Same capability. Different implementations. One design standard.</h3><p>Standardise the control design, minimum requirements and evidence model while retaining implementation-specific execution where needed.</p></div></div></section>

<section class="slide"><div class="tech-hud"><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="data-dot" style="left:8%;top:72%;animation-delay:.7s"></i><i class="data-dot" style="left:48%;top:82%;animation-delay:2.4s"></i></div><div class="tech-ticker"><span>PIPELINE // ACTIVE</span><span>HUMAN VALIDATION // ON</span></div><div class="inner"><div class="eyebrow">09 · Example finding</div><h2>Process-Split Fragmentation</h2><p class="sub">Different stages of one control outcome are registered as separate controls.</p><div class="seq reveal"><div class="card gold"><strong>Daily Nostro and Vostro Account Reconciliation</strong><p>CTL000041583<br><br>Reconciles accounts in TLM and identifies outstanding items.</p><span class="chip">Detect</span></div><div class="seqarr">→</div><div class="card gold"><strong>Daily Nostro Investigation and 6-monthly verification</strong><p>CTL000041584<br><br>Investigates, ages and escalates the resulting exceptions.</p><span class="chip">Investigate</span><span class="chip">Escalate</span></div></div><div class="finding goldline"><div class="type">Process-Split Fragmentation</div><h3>One end-to-end outcome split across registered controls.</h3><p>The opportunity is to redesign the controls as a coherent detect-to-resolution outcome, while preserving the distinct six-monthly verification element where appropriate.</p></div></div></section>

<section class="slide"><div class="tech-hud"><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="data-dot" style="left:8%;top:72%;animation-delay:.7s"></i><i class="data-dot" style="left:48%;top:82%;animation-delay:2.4s"></i></div><div class="tech-ticker"><span>PIPELINE // ACTIVE</span><span>HUMAN VALIDATION // ON</span></div><div class="inner"><div class="eyebrow">10 · Example finding</div><h2>Redundant Control</h2><p class="sub">The same risk and outcome are addressed in the same context, with little incremental mitigation.</p><div class="example reveal"><div class="card red"><strong>10.3.04 Scheduled Process Monitoring and Resolution</strong><p>CTL000156634<br><br>Generic monitoring of batch success and timely resolution of failures within an SLA.</p><span class="chip">Limited design</span></div><div class="plus">vs</div><div class="card teal"><strong>10.3.04 Scheduled Process Monitoring and Resolution</strong><p>CTL000156989<br><br>Includes batch success/failure reporting, incident tickets and root-cause review.</p><span class="chip">Broader evidence</span><span class="chip">Best coverage</span></div></div><div class="finding redline"><div class="type">High-confidence redundancy finding</div><h3>CTL000156634 adds little documented mitigation beyond CTL000156989.</h3><p>The stronger control covers the same scheduled-process monitoring outcome with more explicit reporting, incident and root-cause evidence, making the weaker generic control a consolidation candidate subject to owner validation.</p></div></div></section>

<section class="slide"><div class="tech-hud"><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="data-dot" style="left:8%;top:72%;animation-delay:.7s"></i><i class="data-dot" style="left:48%;top:82%;animation-delay:2.4s"></i></div><div class="tech-ticker"><span>PIPELINE // ACTIVE</span><span>HUMAN VALIDATION // ON</span></div><div class="inner"><div class="eyebrow">11 · What the PoC surfaced</div><h2>Rationalisation opportunities and coverage gaps.</h2><div class="storyline"><span>Scope</span><b>→</b><span>Connect</span><b>→</b><span>Compare</span><b>→</b><span>Challenge</span><b>→</b><span>Recommend</span></div><div class="outcomes reveal"><div class="outcome"><b>163</b><h3>Controls in consolidation opportunities</h3><p>Fragmented or repeated control designs identified for targeted review.</p></div><div class="outcome"><b>497</b><h3>Payments processes with no linked JUNO control</h3><p>111 include AI-recommended associations for investigation, not confirmed control coverage.</p></div><div class="outcome"><b>553</b><h3>Payments processes without an application</h3><p>A material technology-context and data-quality gap.</p></div><div class="outcome"><b>198</b><h3>Payments processes without a product</h3><p>Limits product-level coverage analysis and accountability.</p></div></div><div class="ribbon"><span class="pill">708 Payments controls</span><span class="pill">469 mapped to Holocentric</span><span class="pill">661 identified non-Payments controls</span><span class="pill">808 Payments processes</span></div></div></section>

<section class="slide"><div class="tech-hud"><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="hud-line"></i><i class="data-dot" style="left:8%;top:72%;animation-delay:.7s"></i><i class="data-dot" style="left:48%;top:82%;animation-delay:2.4s"></i></div><div class="tech-ticker"><span>PIPELINE // ACTIVE</span><span>HUMAN VALIDATION // ON</span></div><div class="inner"><div class="eyebrow">12 · What this enables</div><h2>From inventory review to a repeatable control intelligence capability.</h2><div class="close-grid reveal"><div class="close"><b>Rationalise</b><p>Target duplicate, fragmented and common control designs with evidence-led recommendations.</p></div><div class="close"><b>Assess coverage</b><p>Identify Payments processes without evidenced control linkage and prioritise validation.</p></div><div class="close"><b>Improve data</b><p>Expose missing product, application and process relationships that limit risk insight.</p></div></div><div class="govern"><strong>Decision support, not automated decisions:</strong> the PoC produces transparent candidates and rationales for control owners, Risk and SMEs to validate.</div></div></section>
